{"seq":1,"at":"2026-09-17","kind":"imported","target":{"type":"article","id":"a1867"},"reason":"Article of the Quiztopia bank (written for card c156): researched and transcribed in September 2026, converted to the quiz platform on 2026-10-20.","by":"Quiztopia import","ref":"quiz-archive/transcripts/quiztopia/c156.json"},
{"seq":2,"at":"2026-10-20","kind":"edited","target":{"type":"article","id":"a1867"},"reason":"Rewritten by the merge to take in the printed cards' questions.","by":"merge society/business","ref":"quiz-archive/runs/tp-ingest/decisions/society__business.json","changes":[{"field":"bodyEn","diff":[["=",448],["+","The name itself outlived the Austrian coin. Like the British shilling and the old German Schilling, it descends from the Roman solidus, and it still circulates on one continent: Africa. Kenya, Tanzania, Uganda and Somalia all pay in shillings, heirs of the East African shilling that Britain introduced in its colonies in the early 1920s and that was split into national currencies from 1966.\n\n"],["=",535]]},{"field":"bodyDe","diff":[["=",406],["+","Der Name selbst überlebte die österreichische Münze. Wie der britische Shilling und der alte deutsche Schilling geht er auf den römischen Solidus zurück, und er ist noch auf einem Kontinent im Umlauf: in Afrika. Kenia, Tansania, Uganda und Somalia zahlen mit Schilling, Erben des Ostafrikanischen Schillings, den Großbritannien Anfang der 1920er-Jahre in seinen Kolonien einführte und der ab 1966 in nationale Währungen aufgeteilt wurde.\n\n"],["=",515]]}]},
